From a85db0f8acfd52bde11ae2caf70013b8b12d100e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 05:08:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: settings for the colour format and the depth
 of the link, "Automatic" by default

Two expert settings (level 3). "Colour format" (videoscreen.hadescolorformat: Automatic, RGB,
YCbCr 4:4:4, 4:2:2, 4:2:0) sets the connector property "color format" of Linux 7.3, which
amdgpu attaches to its HDMI and DisplayPort connectors (i915, xe and nvidia-drm do not have it):
the setting is shown only where it is there. "Maximum bit depth" (videoscreen.hadesmaxbpc:
Automatic, 8, 10, 12) sets "max bpc" where Kodi can (not nvidia-drm, which has its own setting
for HDR), inside the range of the connector.

Automatic adds nothing to any request, as before; the only difference is that a colour format
that another program left on the connector is put back to AUTO with the mode set that Kodi makes
anyway. A choice goes out only in a request that sets a mode (a change of either is a mode set in
the kernel). A mode that the driver cannot carry as chosen must not stop the change of mode of a
video: CDRMAtomic::AddSoftProperty() tests the refused request again with the values that the
output had, the commit goes out as with Automatic (the frame stays on the screen, the previous
mode is not lost), the log says it, and the choice is not asked again for that mode and value.
Native Dolby Vision (an RGB tunnel at 8 bits, which amdgpu refuses with any other format) captures
"color format", sets AUTO with its transport and puts back what it found; the HDR 4:2:0 rule of
the driver (DVBRIDGE_HDR_YCBCR420) is set to "never" while a format is chosen.

Idea from CroqueMr's colour-format setting (Kodi PRs 30099 and 30192 by smp79), read as data and
made on the property of the kernel. Decisions in OutputFormat.h, host test in both directions (22
checks and a mutation it catches). Strings 41310-41322 (core) and 32910-32922 (Estuary), English
and Spanish. Not tried on hardware.
